::ENG3_U06_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which slide design best improves audience experience? {
~A. A full essay paragraph in tiny text.#Incorrect. This overloads the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. A clear title, one relevant visual, and a short evidence phrase.#Correct. This supports understanding without overload. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Five unrelated images and no claim.#Incorrect. This distracts from the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A blank slide for every point.#Incorrect. Slides should support the presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: How should a digital slide support oral delivery? {
~A. It should contain the full script.#Incorrect. Full scripts on slides overload readers. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. It should contradict the speaker.#Incorrect. Slide and speech should align. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. It should distract from the focus.#Incorrect. Slides should support focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. It should highlight key ideas while the speaker explains details aloud.#Correct. Slides support, not replace, the speaker. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which title best supports complex text analysis? {
=A. Imagery Reveals Character Conflict.#Correct. This title previews the analytical relationship. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Stuff About the Text.#Incorrect. This is vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Slide 3.#Incorrect. This does not help the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Pictures and Words.#Incorrect. This does not preview analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.04] Context: Feedback says the slide has too much text. What revision is best? {
~A. Add smaller text to fit more information.#Incorrect. This makes reading harder. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Add more full sentences.#Incorrect. This increases overload. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Keep key phrases and move details into speaker notes.#Correct. This improves clarity and audience experience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Remove the title and keep the paragraph.#Incorrect. The title helps guide the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: Which visual choice is most audience-centered? {
~A. A decorative image unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect. Decoration without purpose distracts. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. A simple diagram showing claim, evidence, and explanation.#Correct. This visual clarifies the presentation structure. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. A crowded collage of random photos.#Incorrect. This creates visual clutter. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. An image so small no one can see it.#Incorrect. The audience cannot use it. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.C.02] Context: A slide uses yellow text on a white background. What audience-experience issue appears? {
=A. Low readability.#Correct. Poor contrast makes text hard to read. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Too much credible evidence.#Incorrect. The issue is readability. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Strong organization.#Incorrect. Color contrast is not organization. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Effective paraphrasing.#Incorrect. The issue is visual design. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: A complex analysis slide should help the audience see what relationship? {
